# Regression: `Shared[T] !s` where T is a trivial-only struct — a `!` consuming
# call (`take_and_drop(!s)`) must decrement the strong count, and a matched
# `w.upgrade()` must see the control block freed and return None. The original
# bug symptom was "upgrade returns Some instead of None", but the real cause
# turned out to be unrelated to the drop chain — `Option[Shared[T]]` had only
# a Named TypeId, no enum TypeDef, so `resolve_variant_tag` returned None for
# Some/None and `lower_pattern_condition` fell through to const_bool(true).
# Every arm matched, only the first arm body fired. Fix: `Weak.upgrade()`
# lowering now registers the Option's enum TypeDef via
# `ensure_option_type_registered` before the Named registration short-circuits.

struct Cell:
    int val

void take_and_drop(Shared[Cell] !s):
    pass

void main():
    Shared[Cell] s = Shared[Cell](Cell(42))
    Weak[Cell] w = s.downgrade()
    take_and_drop(!s)
    match w.upgrade():
        case Some(alive): print(1)
        case None(): print(0)
